nigeria's central bank is absolutely  lost their minds i love nigeria i love  nigerians i went to nigeria a couple  years ago and in nigeria the two things  that stuck out to me is one the country  of i think 200 million people or so is  growing at an incredible rate it is  estimated that nigeria will be larger on  a population basis than the united  states by the year 2050. it's a very  young population it's a very fast  growing population and there is great  internet penetration in the country  there are a ton of digital natives that  are growing up in nigeria they  understand the internet they understand  smartphones and they are trying to use  the internet to build a better life very  similar to india what we are watching is  that the nigerian population continues  to push that country forward and to make  a better life for themselves using the  internet and using technology now one of  the things that they want is bitcoin we  have seen that nigeria is one of the  most popular countries in the world on a  per capita basis for bitcoin ownership  and usage we've also seen an explosion  of interest in peer-to-peer exchanges  and also with mining after nigeria  banned bitcoin ownership it doesn't  matter the people want it the people  need it the local currency the naira  continues to suffer and in some cases  there is 15 plus percent inflation in  nigeria so if you have a macro  environment that is pushing people out  of the local currency they're trying to  find another asset and then along comes  a digital currency that is decentralized  that can't be debased it can't be  censored and you don't need permission  to buy it or to use it from anyone else  of course it's going to become  attractive if your entire population or  a very big portion of your population  are young people who are technology  savvy but nigeria  is not going to allow that to just  happen overnight the nigerian central  bank is launching a central bank digital  currency they call it the e naira very  clever electronic naira  now the e naira is not meant to replace  the existing currency it is meant to  complement their physical currency and  specifically not to replace it  you can use it with basically two  separate applications they have built an  e-nira speed wallet and they have built  an e-nira merchant wallet so they have  literally built something for consumers  and they have built a piece of  technology for merchants the idea is to  take the central bank currency not  change the monetary policy in any way  and simply to upgrade the technology  where most of it is in physical form and  there's a very high percent of people  who are unbanked and now start to bring  them into the digital world the exact  same monetary policy but the technology  ends up being different now there are  over  500 million inira about 1.2 million  already minted 200 million have been  issued to financial institutions and 33  banks 2 000 customers and 120 merchants  had already registered successfully with  the platform so when you start to look  at this it is very clear that there is a  possibility that you are going to get  the incumbents to start to adopt this  but the question always becomes when you  start to hand digital wallets to  individuals where will they store their  wealth i personally believe that one of  the things that people are drastically  underestimating  is that as the central bank digital  currencies come online  all of these countries are going to go  and they're going to do massive  campaigns they're going to tell people  go get a digital wallet go get a digital  wallet here we'll give you a digital  wallet we'll pay you to get a digital  wallet but when you digitize every  currency in the world whether it's the  naira the dollar the euro the yen or the  peso every single currency will be  digital and when those currencies are  all digital there is no competition at a  technology layer there is no i use the e  naira because it's digital and i can't  use the dollar because i can only get  physical dollars everything is digital  so the competition moves from a  technology competition up to a monetary  policy competition and that monetary  policy competition every single fiat  currency in the world is exactly the  same on a relative basis they have  little differences in their interest  rates or in their contraction or  expansion of their money supplies  but from a macro standpoint every fiat  currency is the exact same there's an  unlimited supply there's a small group  of people who determine the monetary  policy and there is no predict uh  predictability to it you don't know  what's going to happen in the future and  there is very little to any transparency  around the monetary policy and the  actual execution of the currency's  specific aspects  bitcoin is 180 degree difference  it's a finite supply there will only  ever be 21 million it's completely  transparent you know everything that  happens i can go back to january 3rd  2009 and show you every single bitcoin  that has been created who has held it  all the way up till today exactly how  many bitcoin are coming into the supply  today everything everything is  transparent and the monetary policy ends  up being 100 predictable you can know it  with certainty because it's written in a  code and no one person can change it  and so ultimately bitcoin is 180 degree  difference now the big bet that i think  people are willing to take  is that ultimately for a global store of  value forget a medium of exchange permit  for a store of value  you have one asset that is going to be  depreciated over time it actually is  easier to use today in terms of a medium  of exchange for the moment  but from a global store value standpoint  if the fiat currencies are going to  continue to be devalued at a historic  pace all around the world  people especially in places like nigeria  that are experiencing high levels of  inflation they're going to choose  something that can't be 